   "date": "2017-09-09T14:03:48Z",
   "subject": "Prediction of future data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi BRMS users and developers!<div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve built a model in BRMS to represent a meta-analysis with a number of moderators.  It looks like this:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#000\">yi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> se</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sei</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pub_year </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mod_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mod_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">|</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> id</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br><br></div><div><br></div><div>I would like to predict the i<font size=\"1\">+1th</font> study based on draws from the posterior distribution.  Is this possible using BRMS?  I thought it would be a matter of using predict() -  for example - I tried following the manual example:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"font-size:13.333333015441895px\"><span style=\"color:#000\">newdata </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pub_year </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2017</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2018</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                      mod_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#006666\"><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><font color=\"#006666\"><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                      mod_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                      </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>predict</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> newdata </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> newdata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></pre></div></code></div> </div><div>but I get this error:</div><div><div>&quot;Error: The following variables are missing in &#39;data&#39;: &#39;sei&#39;&quot;</div></div><div><br></div><div>The the sei is unknown so I&#39;m not sure what to enter - does anyone have any clues at all?</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks in advance!</div><div><br></div><div>Oliver</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
